<header><p class="enc-kicker"><a href="/encyclopedia/glossary#tigrinya-language">Glossary</a> · Term</p><h1 class="enc-title">Tigrinya language</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as Tigrinya</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Tigrinya is a Semitic language of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/horn-of-africa">Horn of Africa</a>, spoken in the highlands of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/eritrea">Eritrea</a> and in the Tigray region of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ethiopia">Ethiopia</a>. It descends from the same branch as <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/geez">Ge'ez</a>, the ancient language of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/aksumite-empire">Aksum</a>, and is written in the Ge'ez script. It is one of the main working languages of Eritrea.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/tigrinya-language.jpg" alt="Tigrinya language" width="320" height="320" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: MarginalCost · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Eritrea_Ethiopia_locator.svg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Origins</h2><p>Tigrinya is an Ethio-Semitic language, a subgroup of the Semitic branch of the Afroasiatic languages. It is spoken mainly by the Tigrinya and Tigrayan peoples of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/eritrea">Eritrea</a> and the Tigray Region of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ethiopia">Ethiopia</a>, and by diaspora communities. It differs markedly from Geʽez, the classical Ethiopic language, for instance in having phrasal verbs and in placing the main verb last in the sentence. Even so, Geʽez strongly influenced Tigrinya literature, especially in terms relating to Christian life and in Biblical names, and its lexical similarity to Geʽez is 68 per cent, slightly higher than <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/amharic">Amharic</a>'s 62 per cent.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">What it is</h2><p>Tigrinya has a rich oral tradition but historically lacked a written literary tradition, which Carlo Conti Rossini attributed to the use of Geʽez in church texts and learned circles and to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/amharic">Amharic</a>'s status as the language of the ruling authorities. The earliest known example of Tigrinya literature is a text of local laws from the Loggo Sarda district of present-day <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/eritrea">Eritrea</a>, believed to date from the 19th century and inserted into a Geʽez manuscript of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gospels">Gospels</a> from about the late 15th or early 16th century.</p><p>Its sounds include ejective consonants and a seven-vowel system, and unlike many related languages it kept the two pharyngeal consonants that were apparently part of Geʽez. Verbs are built on consonantal roots, usually of three consonants, and the usual word order is subject, object, verb. Unusually among Ethiopian Semitic languages, it has a definite article. Dialects differ, and none is accepted as a standard, though the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/asmara">Asmara</a> dialect is the most widely used in books, films and news.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">How it was used</h2><p>Tigrinya is written in the Geʽez script, an abugida in which each symbol stands for a consonant and a vowel. The orthography does not mark gemination. Under British administration in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/eritrea">Eritrea</a>, the Ministry of Information issued a weekly Tigrinya newspaper that sold 5,000 copies. Tigrinya and Arabic were official languages of Eritrea during its federation with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ethiopia">Ethiopia</a>, until <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/amharic">Amharic</a> replaced Tigrinya in 1958. After independence in 1991 Tigrinya kept the status of a working language. It is the most widely spoken language in Eritrea and the fourth most spoken in Ethiopia, after Amharic, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/oromo">Oromo</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/somalis">Somali</a>. Ethiopia recognised it at national level in 2020.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Tigrinya language” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Ge'ez, the language of Aksumite inscriptions, became the liturgical and literary language of the Ethiopian Orthodox Church, and its script, which the Aksumites developed into a fully vocalised alphabet, is still used to write Amharic, <b>Tigrinya</b> and other languages today.</blockquote><a href="/empire/aksumite-empire">Aksumite Empire · Global &amp; Regional Impact</a></li><li><blockquote>On another view the Semitic languages of the two countries form a branch of their own, with Ge'ez, <b>Tigrinya</b> and Tigre in its northern half and Amharic, Argobba, Harari and the Gurage languages in its southern.</blockquote><a href="/language/geez">Ge'ez · Origins</a></li><li><blockquote>The term belongs to Amharic and <b>Tigrinya</b> and has no equivalent in Ge'ez.</blockquote><a href="/songs/tizita">Tizita</a></li><li><blockquote>The British kept much of the Italian administration and allowed political parties, trade unions and newspapers in <b>Tigrinya</b> and Arabic.</blockquote><a href="/state/british-administration-of-eritrea">British Administration of Eritrea</a></li><li><blockquote>It received its own constitution, an elected assembly in Asmara, an executive and official languages, <b>Tigrinya</b> and Arabic, while Ethiopia controlled defence, foreign affairs, currency and trade.</blockquote><a href="/state/eritrea-federation">Eritrea (federation)</a></li></ul></section>
